CLINERULES: Automated Fantasy Football Draft & Season Assistant Architecture
Operating Principles

    Absolute Execution Autonomy: Build modular, fail-safe code designed to run without manual user input during live drafts.

    Low Latency Performance: All local calculation loops (Dynamic VORP, Make-it-back probabilities) must complete in under 50ms per pick event.

    Strict System Separation: Keep data collection (Python sidecar), desktop wrapper (Tauri/Rust), UI (React), and extension hook strictly decoupled.

Tech Stack Standards

    Frontend: React 18+, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, Lucide React, Zustand for state management.

    Desktop Shell: Tauri v2 (Rust backend handling IPC, local WebSocket server, and Python process management).

    Analytics Engine: Python 3.11+, pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, nfl_data_py, espn-api, yfpy.

    Browser Extension: Chrome Extension Manifest v3, TypeScript, WebSockets.

Mathematical Constraints

    Dynamic VORP must update replacement thresholds dynamically based on remaining roster slots across all league teams.

    Make-It-Back probability calculations must handle normal distributions using aggregated ADP standard deviations.

    Draft decision rankings must output a utility score calculated via weighted multi-variable optimization.

Code Style & Safety

    Use explicit TypeScript interfaces for all draft events, player records, and league settings.

    Implement failover handling for platform integrations: if WebSockets fail, automatically fallback to REST polling every 3 seconds.

    All Python code must execute within an isolated virtual environment packaged inside the Tauri sidecar structure.